      "question": {
        ":question/id": "738dee33-9503-50b6-8825-d5a91cfc9509",
        ":question/problem": "Find ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{7}\u2061(x).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)^{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{7},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=7{u}^{6}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =7{u}^{6}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =-7{u}^{6}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\  & =-7(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)^{6}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\  & =-7{cos}^{6}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
